WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.342
A taxonomy field stores the terms an editor picked.

00:00:04.342 --> 00:00:08.094
This video queries content items by those terms from code:

00:00:08.094 --> 00:00:14.660
through the TaxonomyIndex table, through a Lucene query with parameters, and through the term page filter.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.205
The TaxonomyField class has two properties:

00:00:22.205 --> 00:00:26.411
the content item id of its taxonomy, and the content item ids of

00:00:26.411 --> 00:00:28.515
the terms an editor selected.

00:00:28.515 --> 00:00:31.880
Every query in this video works on those term ids.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.756
The TaxonomyIndex table has one row for each selected term of each field.

00:00:37.756 --> 00:00:42.428
A row names the taxonomy, the content item, its type, the part and

00:00:42.428 --> 00:00:46.840
the field, and the term, plus two flags, Published and Latest.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.058
In a view, the Orchard helper provides QueryCategorizedContentItemsAsync.

00:00:56.058 --> 00:01:00.950
You pass a function that receives a query on TaxonomyIndex, and you get

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.300
back the content items, loaded.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.947
This TaxonomyField view lists related items.

00:01:06.947 --> 00:01:10.828
It reads the term ids of the field, keeps the rows whose term

00:01:10.828 --> 00:01:14.420
is one of them, and leaves out the item being displayed.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.417
The helper adds no filter of its own.

00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:21.024
The index has rows for the published version of an item and for

00:01:21.024 --> 00:01:25.048
the version flagged Latest, so add Published or Latest to the query, as

00:01:25.048 --> 00:01:26.930
this view does with Published.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.903
The index holds only the terms an editor selected.

00:01:30.903 --> 00:01:34.644
An item filed under a child term has no row for its parent,

00:01:34.644 --> 00:01:37.810
so a query on the parent term does not return it.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.918
Outside a view, run the same query on ISession, and load the results

00:01:42.918 --> 00:01:44.744
with the content manager.

00:01:44.744 --> 00:01:49.220
Use it in a service, a controller, or any code that is not a template.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.031
A Lucene index can hold the same ids.

00:01:56.031 --> 00:02:00.282
For a field marked Include this element in the index, the key is

00:02:00.282 --> 00:02:04.533
the type or part name, the field name, then Ids for the selected

00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:08.420
terms, or Inherited for those terms and all their parents.

00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:16.845
In Design, Content Definition, Content Types, open the Blog Post type and edit its Category field.

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:22.413
Under Lucene Index settings, check Include this element in the index, and save.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.968
Do the same for the Tags field, so both fields write their term

00:02:26.968 --> 00:02:28.330
ids to the index.

00:02:28.782 --> 00:02:31.858
A rebuild indexes the existing posts again.

00:02:31.858 --> 00:02:36.002
Go to Search, Indexes, and open Actions on the Search index.

00:02:36.472 --> 00:02:37.693
Choose Rebuild.

00:02:37.693 --> 00:02:43.452
A notification says the index was rebuilt, and the posts are indexed again in the background.

00:02:47.652 --> 00:02:51.310
A Lucene query is JSON, and Liquid renders it first.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.652
The clause for the content type always applies.

00:02:54.652 --> 00:02:58.941
An if tag adds a match clause for categories or tags only when

00:02:58.941 --> 00:03:01.022
that parameter has a value.

00:03:01.772 --> 00:03:05.937
A match query splits its text into ids and joins them with or.

00:03:05.937 --> 00:03:09.918
The must list joins the clauses with and, so a post needs a

00:03:09.918 --> 00:03:12.552
matching category and a matching tag.

00:03:14.143 --> 00:03:18.915
On the Lucene Query page, the template runs against the Search index.

00:03:18.915 --> 00:03:22.923
Without parameters, it returns the blog posts, up to ten.

00:03:23.378 --> 00:03:26.787
Save Query opens the form for a Lucene query.

00:03:26.787 --> 00:03:32.668
Name it GetBlogsByFilter, select the Search index, and check Return Content Items.

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:33.978
Save it.

00:03:33.978 --> 00:03:38.769
The query appears in All Queries, and Razor or Liquid can run it by name.

00:03:39.232 --> 00:03:44.220
In All Queries, Run opens the query on the Lucene Query page again.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.482
Parameters takes a JSON object, with one value for each parameter name.

00:03:49.951 --> 00:03:54.659
Passing the id of the Politics term as categories returns two posts.

00:03:54.659 --> 00:03:58.731
The table shows the stored fields, including the term ids.

00:04:02.931 --> 00:04:06.672
In Razor, ContentQueryAsync runs the query by name.

00:04:06.672 --> 00:04:11.726
Pass the parameters in a dictionary, join several ids with a space, and

00:04:11.726 --> 00:04:14.351
leave out a filter you do not use.

00:04:15.101 --> 00:04:20.412
ContentQueryAsync returns content items, so a view can list their DisplayText.

00:04:20.412 --> 00:04:26.671
In Liquid, the query filter on the Queries property runs the same query with named arguments.

00:04:30.871 --> 00:04:34.352
A term page lists its items with the same index.

00:04:34.352 --> 00:04:39.315
It asks TaxonomyIndex for the rows of the page's term, then calls every

00:04:39.315 --> 00:04:44.021
registered IContentsTaxonomyListFilter before it applies the pager.

00:04:44.771 --> 00:04:47.749
A filter receives the query and the TermPart.

00:04:47.749 --> 00:04:52.071
Implement it to narrow or change the query before the list is paged.

00:04:54.071 --> 00:04:58.604
You can query content items by term from the TaxonomyIndex, from a Lucene

00:04:58.604 --> 00:05:01.989
query with parameters, and from the term page filter.

00:05:01.989 --> 00:05:03.481
Thanks for watching.

